From 3422a9c6b78f21faf013787d66ba7e51f15ca822 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 11:58:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Turn to whoever hit us

A blow was delivered as a prompt to whichever glance was running, and that is
the one answer to it a look cannot give. The walking glance refuses an interest
further off than its own and is only advanced at all on a walk the executor has
welcomed, so a bot punched from the side or from behind while walking - which is
where somebody sneaking up on a bot actually is - did not turn its head at all;
a standing one stepped round at the speed of a look and took the better part of
a second over it. Both contradict the doctrine the blow path was written with:
a blow says where it came from, whether or not it was in view.

New Flinch (behavior/look/Flinch.java, Minecraft-free, FlinchTest beside it): a
drawn reaction of two to six ticks, then the turn at the hand's own ceiling - a
flick, which is what a startle is - unbounded while the feet are still, because
a standing body turns whole, and stopped at a neck's quarter turn while the feet
are moving, because a walking body cannot come round without walking backwards.
Held ten to thirty ticks, drawn when the blow lands and counted from arrival,
with the attacker asked for again every tick so somebody walking about is
tracked rather than remembered; handed back at the speed of a look with the sway
still riding, because coming home is not a flinch. It stands down wherever the
sway does, and for anything being clicked exactly as the glances do: the head
comes home rather than vanishing, and a stare that is still owed is taken up
again if the head comes free in time. A second blow on the heels of the first is
the same flinch held again, at whoever it names last, and draws itself no second
reaction.

LookBehavior routes noticeBlows into it instead of into the glances, and adds
its offsets wherever the sway's and the glance's offsets are added, on the same
base, so it is handed back with them; it is reset with the rest of the head's
state on a world event. Newcomer loses the blow path entirely - a blow is not an
arrival and not a look - and NewcomerTest the four tests of it.

Validation: ./gradlew :test green - 297 tests, 0 failures (10 of them
FlinchTest). HumanlikeLookFuzzTest does not model the blow and now says so where
its gates are: the flinch is advanced with the sway's own three flags, so those
gates are its gates, and its angles are measured in FlinchTest instead.
